#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "80368ff1-7296-5f6c-aa32-63547e29e578" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083001 :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083002 :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083003 :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082895 :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 758 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048618 :topic/math-academy-id 4005 :topic/title "Improper Integrals Involving Arctangent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/math-academy-id 3289 :topic/title "The Gamma Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049648 :topic/math-academy-id 744 :topic/title "The Integral Test"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015 :topic/math-academy-id 4004 :topic/title "Improper Integrals Involving Exponential Functions"}] :topic/title "Improper Integrals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048150 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050050 :topic/math-academy-id 1060 :topic/title "Expressing Rational Functions as Sums of Partial Fractions"}] :topic/id #uuid "83842e35-06ae-5693-b3dc-b06146867370" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068584 :question/id #uuid "f374b567-9a69-57ae-88eb-644ebfc451d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-506" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068586 :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 445 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048503 :topic/math-academy-id 447 :topic/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Irreducible Quadratic Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049227 :topic/math-academy-id 446 :topic/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Repeated Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050913 :topic/math-academy-id 3628 :topic/title "Further Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution"}] :topic/title "Integrating Rational Functions Using Partial Fractions"}]}
